httpd used a range_end of 0 to mean that the Range header gave no last
byte, as in "bytes=500-", since that is its value when the globals are
zeroed. But 0 is also a valid last byte, so "bytes=0-0", a request for
the first byte, was answered with the whole file, and a Content-Range
claiming that whole file.

Start range_end at the same "no limit" value used when range support is
compiled out, so that an open range is cut back to the end of the file
by the existing check against the file size, and an explicit last byte
of 0 is honored.

---
 networking/httpd.c |    3 ++-
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/networking/httpd.c
+++ b/networking/httpd.c
@@ -639,6 +639,7 @@
 	SET_OFFSET_PTR_TO_GLOBALS(xzalloc(sizeof(G))); \
 	IF_FEATURE_HTTPD_BASIC_AUTH(g_realm = "Web Server Authentication";) \
 	IF_FEATURE_HTTPD_RANGES(range_start = -1;) \
+	IF_FEATURE_HTTPD_RANGES(range_end = MAXINT(off_t) - 1;) \
 	bind_addr_or_port = STR(CONFIG_FEATURE_HTTPD_PORT_DEFAULT); \
 	index_page = index_html; \
 	file_size = -1; \
@@ -2136,7 +2137,7 @@
 	}
